module

public import Mathlib.Algebra.Module.Torsion.Basic
public import Mathlib.LinearAlgebra.LinearIndependent.Lemmas
public import Mathlib.RingTheory.LocalRing.ResidueField.Basic
public import StacksCore.Tactic.StacksAttribute

/-! # Detecting a minimal system of generators after a linear map

Stacks Tag 0FCW runs through Rodicio's theorem (Stacks Tag 09PV) applied to a pair of ideals
`I ⊆ J` of the local ring `S = (R ⊗_A R)_𝔪`, where `B = R ⧸ K` and `I` is generated by the `2r`
elements `f₁ ⊗ 1, …, f_r ⊗ 1, 1 ⊗ f₁, …, 1 ⊗ f_r` for a system of generators `f₁, …, f_r` of `K`.
The rank step of that proof — `StacksDraft.Rodicio.rank_residue_eq_of_injective_mapQ` — takes as a
hypothesis that those `2r` elements are a *minimal* system of generators, spelled as linear
independence over the residue field of their classes in `I ⧸ 𝔪I`. The source establishes it like
this:

> Namely, if `κ` is the common residue field of `R`, `B`, `(R ⊗_A R)_𝔪` and `(B ⊗_A B)_𝔪` then we
> have a map `R ⊗_A R → R ⊗_A κ ⊕ κ ⊗_A R`. … Thus restricting the map to `I` we obtain a map
> `I → K ⊗_B κ ⊕ κ ⊗_B K`. The elements `f₁ ⊗ 1, …, 1 ⊗ f_r` map to a basis of the target of this
> map.

This file proves the step the source's paragraph *is*, with the pair of maps abstracted: a system
of generators is minimal as soon as there are two linear maps out of `I`, each separating one of
the two families and killing the other. Nothing of the configuration enters, so the statement is
about a local ring, an ideal and two linear maps and nothing else.

## Main definitions

* `StacksDraft.Rodicio.mapQMaximalIdealSMulTop`: the map `M ⧸ 𝔪M → N ⧸ 𝔪N` induced by an
  `S`-linear map `M → N`, as a map linear over the residue ring `S ⧸ 𝔪`.

## Main results

* `StacksDraft.Rodicio.linearIndependent_sum_elim_mk_of_separating`: the minimality step. Given
  `u, v : Fin r → I` and `S`-linear maps `p : I → N₁` and `q : I → N₂` such that `p` carries `u`
  to a linearly independent family of `N₁ ⧸ 𝔪N₁` while killing `v` modulo `𝔪N₁`, and symmetrically
  for `q`, the `2r` classes of `u` and `v` in `I ⧸ 𝔪I` are linearly independent.

## Implementation notes

The residue field is spelled as the plain quotient ring `S ⧸ IsLocalRing.maximalIdeal S` rather
than as `IsLocalRing.ResidueField S`, for the reason recorded in
`StacksFrontier/RingTheory/DividedPowers/Rodicio/RankStep.lean`: the `S ⧸ 𝔪`-module structure on
`M ⧸ 𝔪 • ⊤` is found by instance search (it is `Module.IsTorsionBySet.module` of
`Module.isTorsionBySet_quotient_ideal_smul`), while no such instance exists for `ResidueField`.
This is also the spelling in which the rank step states its hypothesis, so no transport is needed
between the two.

The two external results the source cites for this step are both already in Mathlib, and are *not*
restated here:

* Stacks 00HL, that `K ⊗_A κ → R ⊗_A κ` is injective when `R ⧸ K` is flat over `A`, is
  `LinearMap.lTensor_injective_of_exact_of_flat`;
* Stacks 00DV in the form the step uses, that lifts of a basis of `κ ⊗ K` generate `K`, is
  `IsLocalRing.span_eq_top_of_tmul_eq_basis`.

What this step still needs, to become the source's own minimality argument for Tag 0FCW, is the
construction of the two maps `p` and `q` for the specific configuration. Tag 0FCW itself does not
wait on it: `StacksDraft.Smooth.exists_smooth_isLocalization_of_hasProjectiveDimensionLT_diagonal`
and its perfect-diagonal form
`StacksDraft.Smooth.exists_smooth_isLocalization_of_isPerfect_diagonal` reach the same conclusion
by transporting Rodicio's injectivity along a single comparison map rather than through a rank
count, so this file records the source's route independently.

One warning for whoever builds the two maps. The source says the map
`R ⊗_A R → R ⊗_A κ ⊕ κ ⊗_A R` "factors through `(R ⊗_A R)_𝔪`", and that is not correct as it
stands, because `R ⊗_A κ` need not be local: for `A` a field `k` and `R = B = k(y)`, so that
`κ = k(y)` and the map is the identity of `k(y) ⊗_k k(y)`, the element `y ⊗ 1 - 1 ⊗ y²` has
nonzero image under the multiplication map and so lies outside `𝔪`, yet it is a non-unit —
`k(y) ⊗_k k(y)` is `k[u, v]` localized at the products of nonzero one-variable polynomials, and
the irreducible `u - v²` divides no such product. The repair is free: localize the target at
`𝔫 = ker (R ⊗_A κ → κ)`. Since `R ⊗_A R → R ⊗_A κ → κ` agrees with
`R ⊗_A R → B ⊗_A B → B → κ`, and `𝔪` is by definition the preimage of the maximal ideal of `B`,
an element outside `𝔪` has nonzero image in `κ` and hence lies outside `𝔫`. The eventual target
`K ⊗_B κ ⊕ κ ⊗_B K` is a `κ`-vector space, so localizing does not change it.

## References

* [Stacks Project, Tag 0FCW](https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/0FCW)
* [Stacks Project, Tag 00DV](https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/00DV)
* [Stacks Project, Tag 00HL](https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/00HL)
-/

@[expose] public noncomputable section

namespace StacksDraft.Rodicio

open IsLocalRing

universe u

variable {S : Type u} [CommRing S] [IsLocalRing S]

/-- The map `M ⧸ 𝔪M → N ⧸ 𝔪N` induced by an `S`-linear map `f : M → N`, linear over the residue
ring `S ⧸ 𝔪`. It is `Submodule.mapQ` of `f`, with its `S`-linearity upgraded along the surjection
`S → S ⧸ 𝔪`. -/
public def mapQMaximalIdealSMulTop {M N : Type*} [AddCommGroup M] [Module S M]
    [AddCommGroup N] [Module S N] (f : M →ₗ[S] N) :
    (M ⧸ (maximalIdeal S • (⊤ : Submodule S M))) →ₗ[S ⧸ maximalIdeal S]
      (N ⧸ (maximalIdeal S • (⊤ : Submodule S N))) where
  toFun := Submodule.mapQ _ _ f (Submodule.smul_top_le_comap_smul_top _ f)
  map_add' x y := by simp
  map_smul' c x := by
    induction c using Quotient.inductionOn' with
    | h a =>
      induction x using Quotient.inductionOn' with
      | h m =>
        change (Submodule.mapQ _ _ f _) (Submodule.Quotient.mk (a • m)) =
          _ • (Submodule.mapQ _ _ f _) (Submodule.Quotient.mk m)
        rw [Submodule.mapQ_apply, Submodule.mapQ_apply, map_smul, RingHom.id_apply]
        rfl

/-- `StacksDraft.Rodicio.mapQMaximalIdealSMulTop` sends the class of `m` to the class of `f m`. -/
@[simp]
public theorem mapQMaximalIdealSMulTop_mk {M N : Type*} [AddCommGroup M] [Module S M]
    [AddCommGroup N] [Module S N] (f : M →ₗ[S] N) (m : M) :
    mapQMaximalIdealSMulTop f (Submodule.Quotient.mk m) = Submodule.Quotient.mk (f m) :=
  rfl

/-- If a relation among the classes of two families `a, b` in `M ⧸ 𝔪M` is pushed through a linear
map `f` that is independent on `a` and kills `b` modulo `𝔪`, the coefficients of `a` vanish. -/
private theorem eq_zero_of_sum_add_sum_eq_zero {M N : Type*} [AddCommGroup M] [Module S M]
    [AddCommGroup N] [Module S N] {r : ℕ} (f : M →ₗ[S] N) (a b : Fin r → M)
    (c₁ c₂ : Fin r → S ⧸ maximalIdeal S)
    (h : ∑ i, c₁ i • (Submodule.Quotient.mk (a i) :
      M ⧸ (maximalIdeal S • (⊤ : Submodule S M))) +
      ∑ i, c₂ i • (Submodule.Quotient.mk (b i) :
      M ⧸ (maximalIdeal S • (⊤ : Submodule S M))) = 0)
    (ha : LinearIndependent (S ⧸ maximalIdeal S) (fun i =>
      (Submodule.Quotient.mk (f (a i)) : N ⧸ (maximalIdeal S • (⊤ : Submodule S N)))))
    (hb : ∀ i, f (b i) ∈ maximalIdeal S • (⊤ : Submodule S N)) (i : Fin r) : c₁ i = 0 := by
  have h' := congrArg (mapQMaximalIdealSMulTop f) h
  simp only [map_add, map_sum, map_smul, mapQMaximalIdealSMulTop_mk, map_zero,
    (Submodule.Quotient.mk_eq_zero _).2 (hb _), smul_zero, Finset.sum_const_zero, add_zero] at h'
  exact Fintype.linearIndependent_iff.mp ha _ h' i

/-- **Minimality of a two-part system of generators, detected by a pair of separating maps.** Let
`I` be an ideal of a local ring `S`, let `u, v : Fin r → I`, and suppose given `S`-linear maps
`p : I → N₁` and `q : I → N₂` such that

* the classes of `p (u i)` in `N₁ ⧸ 𝔪N₁` are linearly independent, while every `p (v i)` lies in
  `𝔪N₁`;
* the classes of `q (v i)` in `N₂ ⧸ 𝔪N₂` are linearly independent, while every `q (u i)` lies in
  `𝔪N₂`.

Then the `2r` classes of `u` and `v` in `I ⧸ 𝔪I` are linearly independent over the residue ring.

This is the hypothesis `hmin` of `StacksDraft.Rodicio.rank_residue_eq_of_injective_mapQ`, and it is
the step Stacks Tag 0FCW performs with `N₁ = K ⊗_B κ` and `N₂ = κ ⊗_B K`: each of the two families
`f₁ ⊗ 1, …, f_r ⊗ 1` and `1 ⊗ f₁, …, 1 ⊗ f_r` is a basis of one summand and dies in the other,
because the `fᵢ` lie in `K` and therefore vanish in `κ`.

A relation among the `2r` classes is pushed through `p`, where the `v` part disappears and linear
independence of the `u` part forces the first `r` coefficients to vanish; `q` does the same for the
other `r`. -/
public theorem linearIndependent_sum_elim_mk_of_separating
    (I : Ideal S) {r : ℕ} (u v : Fin r → I)
    {N₁ N₂ : Type*} [AddCommGroup N₁] [Module S N₁] [AddCommGroup N₂] [Module S N₂]
    (p : ↥I →ₗ[S] N₁) (q : ↥I →ₗ[S] N₂)
    (hpu : LinearIndependent (S ⧸ maximalIdeal S) (fun i : Fin r =>
      (Submodule.Quotient.mk (p (u i)) : N₁ ⧸ (maximalIdeal S • (⊤ : Submodule S N₁)))))
    (hqv : LinearIndependent (S ⧸ maximalIdeal S) (fun i : Fin r =>
      (Submodule.Quotient.mk (q (v i)) : N₂ ⧸ (maximalIdeal S • (⊤ : Submodule S N₂)))))
    (hpv : ∀ i, p (v i) ∈ maximalIdeal S • (⊤ : Submodule S N₁))
    (hqu : ∀ i, q (u i) ∈ maximalIdeal S • (⊤ : Submodule S N₂)) :
    LinearIndependent (S ⧸ maximalIdeal S) (fun k : Fin r ⊕ Fin r =>
      (Submodule.Quotient.mk (Sum.elim u v k) :
        ↥I ⧸ (maximalIdeal S • (⊤ : Submodule S ↥I)))) := by
  rw [Fintype.linearIndependent_iff]
  intro c hc
  rw [Fintype.sum_sum_type] at hc
  simp only [Sum.elim_inl, Sum.elim_inr] at hc
  rintro (i | i)
  · exact eq_zero_of_sum_add_sum_eq_zero p u v _ _ hc hpu hpv i
  · exact eq_zero_of_sum_add_sum_eq_zero q v u _ _ (by rwa [add_comm] at hc) hqv hqu i

end StacksDraft.Rodicio

end
